package secret

// The components: the GM's reveal affordance, the two alerts, the callout's
// rendered chrome, and the opt-in stub.
//
// # Nothing here renders a player's page
//
// The absence is the design, and it is the package comment's subject. These
// components render for **one** reader: the campaign's GM, in the editor or on
// the table. A player's view of a page containing a collapsed callout is
// produced entirely by `internal/content`'s redactor and this package is not in
// that path — so there is no component here a route could mount into a player's
// document and get a secret out of, which is the property `secret_test.go`
// asserts over the real rendered document.
//
// # Where each one mounts
//
//	Disclosures   the editor's preview pane, beside the rendered body
//	RevealOutcome the editor's assertive region, patched by the script
//	CappedAlert   the play document's assertive region, patched by the stream
//	CalloutChrome a revealed callout's own chrome, wherever a body is rendered
//	Stub          the opt-in §4.10.2 stub, when a campaign has enabled it
//
// Five components and no wrapper, for the package comment's reason: a wrapper
// would need a field saying which surface it is on, and that is a field a route
// can be passed the wrong value for.
//
// # The "Revealed" marker is a text node, and the stylesheet carries no colour
//
// §4.10.4: "The marker is a text label, not an icon, because it is the one place
// the UI must be understood without sight *or* without colour perception." So it
// is a `<span>` holding the word, inside a `role="note"`… no: inside nothing
// with a role, because a role here is a thing a screen reader announces and this
// is a label a GM reads while scanning. The class carries the styling and
// `secret.css` styles the text too, so the label survives a monochrome display
// and a `forced-colors` session.

import (
	"strconv"

	"github.com/semiplane/semiplane/internal/web/components/ui"
)

// The test hooks. Named because §4.10's components are asserted against markup
// and a hook is the only stable handle on it (AGENTS.md: "Assert on that, never
// on CSS classes or DOM shape").
const (
	// DisclosuresTestID is the disclosure panel's hook.
	DisclosuresTestID = "secret-disclosures"
	// CalloutControlTestID prefixes each per-callout control's hook, with the
	// ordinal appended — `<ordinal>` is not a secret (it is the callout's
	// position on a GM's own page) and a hook nobody can name is a hook no
	// test can assert on.
	CalloutControlTestID = "secret-callout"
	// RevealAllTestID is the page-level control's hook.
	RevealAllTestID = "secret-reveal-all"
	// OutcomeRegionTestID is the assertive region's hook. **Empty at load** and
	// present, for §7.5's replay rule: a live region that fires on arrival is
	// the search route's mistake.
	OutcomeRegionTestID = "secret-outcome-region"
	// OutcomeTestID prefixes the fragment patched into that region.
	OutcomeTestID = "secret-outcome"
	// CappedTestID is the reconcile-capped fragment's hook.
	CappedTestID = "secret-reconcile-capped"
	// CalloutChromeTestID prefixes a rendered callout's hook.
	CalloutChromeTestID = "secret-callout-chrome"
	// MarkerTestID is the revealed marker's hook.
	MarkerTestID = "secret-revealed-marker"
	// StubTestID is the opt-in stub's hook.
	StubTestID = "secret-stub"
)

// The element id the outcome region's heading carries.
//
// Derived rather than supplied, for `ui.headingID`'s reason: a caller inventing
// one would invent a different one per render, and an `aria-labelledby` naming an
// id that moves is a region with no accessible name.
func outcomeHeadingID() string { return OutcomeRegionTestID + "-heading" }

// The words. Every sentence a reader hears is written here rather than supplied
// by a caller, for the reason `components.LoginFailure` is a closed enum: the
// wording is the interface's, and a sentence invented in a handler is a sentence
// a vocabulary test cannot reach and a copy editor has to find in a route.
//
// Three of them are the whole disclosure surface's copy, and each is quoted from
// the record so a reader can check it:
//
//   - "Revealed. Logged to the audit trail." — §4.10.3, verbatim.
//   - "A secret stayed hidden." — §4.10.3's condition, and `live`'s own
//     `NoticeReconcileCapped` heading. **The same sentence in both packages**, and
//     `TestTheCappedHeadingIsTheOneLiveAlreadyUses` holds them together so a
//     reword cannot reach one.
//   - "Revealed" — §4.10.4's marker word, which is the marker and nothing else.
//
// The hide branch has two words rather than one — a button reading "Hide again" and
// an outcome reading a sentence — because they are read in different positions: one
// is a control in a list of controls, the other is an interruption. §4.10.3's
// reveal outcome is the record's own sentence and is quoted whole.
//
// **`hideLabel` and not `hideSentence` in the button**, and that distinction is
// load-bearing rather than a matter of taste: the button's `aria-label` is built
// from `hideLabel`, so a button whose *visible* text were the sentence would carry
// two different strings, and §10.2's label-in-name rule asks the visible label to be
// contained in the accessible name. "Hidden again. Nobody new can read it." is not
// contained in "Hide again: …", and a sighted GM and a screen-reader user would be
// looking at and hearing two different controls.
// `TestTheVisibleLabelIsInsideTheAccessibleName` holds it.
const (
	revealedSentence = "Revealed. Logged to the audit trail."
	hideSentence     = "Hidden again. Nobody new can read it."
	cappedHeading    = "A secret stayed hidden"
	cappedSentence   = "A sync client kept changing this page back, so semiplane " +
		"stopped trying to re-apply the reveal and left the secret hidden. " +
		"Undo the change on disk, then reveal it again."
	markerWord    = "Revealed"
	revealLabel   = "Reveal"
	hideLabel     = "Hide again"
	revealAllLabel = "Reveal every secret on this page"
	stubSentence  = "A GM-only note"
)

// DisclosuresView is the GM's disclosure panel: one control per callout, plus
// the page-level reveal-all.
//
// # No `If-Match` here, and the validator travels on each control instead
//
// A single panel-level validator would be one value every control shares, which
// is exactly the shape of bug S-6.3 is about: two reveals from two tabs would
// hold one validator for a page two writes have changed. Each control carries
// **its own** `data-secret-if-match`, so a client that fires two of them without
// re-reading gets a 412 on the second and a §4.8 conflict diff rather than a
// silent double-disclosure.
//
// The panel is rendered server-side and the controls carry data attributes
// rather than submitting, for the reason `ui.Dialog` and `edit`'s save control
// both give: the request is a `PUT` with a precondition, and an HTML form can set
// neither a method nor a header. This is the same server-rendered half of a
// contract whose other half is phase 9's script.
type DisclosuresView struct {
	// Endpoint is where every reveal on this page goes. `Endpoint`'s output.
	Endpoint string
	// Page is the page's own name, used in each control's accessible name so
	// three "Reveal" buttons on three open tabs are distinguishable.
	Page string
	// Validator is the page's current `If-Match`. Put on every control; see
	// the type comment for why it is not one panel-level value.
	Validator string
	// Callouts are the page's callouts, in document order. **Non-empty or the
	// panel renders nothing at all** — a disclosure panel with no callouts in
	// it is a heading that says the page has secrets when it does not.
	Callouts []CalloutView
	// Marker decides whether a revealed callout carries the "Revealed" text
	// label. `MarkerOff` is the published page's default; the editor passes
	// `MarkerShown` unconditionally, for the package comment's reason.
	Marker Marker
}

// Disclosures is the GM's disclosure panel.
//
// A `<section>` with its own `<h2>` and an `aria-labelledby` naming it, for
// `live.Notice`'s reason: a landmark a reader cannot jump to is not one, and a
// visible heading is the only version of a name everyone else can read too.
//
// **Nothing at all when there are no callouts.** §4.6's rule that a section with
// nothing in it is absent, applied to a panel whose absence is also the honest
// answer: a GM opening the editor of a page with no secrets should not be told
// there are any, and should not be given a heading with nothing under it.
templ Disclosures(view DisclosuresView) {
	if len(view.Callouts) == 0 {
		// No callouts, no panel. See the type comment.
	} else {
		<section
			class="secret-disclosures"
			aria-labelledby={ DisclosuresTestID + "-heading" }
			data-testid={ DisclosuresTestID }
		>
			<h2 id={ DisclosuresTestID + "-heading" }>Secrets on this page</h2>
			<ul class="secret-disclosure-list">
				for _, callout := range view.Callouts {
					@calloutControl(callout, view)
				}
			</ul>
			@revealAllControl(view)
		</section>
	}
}

// calloutControl is one callout's control: its name, its state, and the button
// that flips it.
//
// **The button carries `.target` itself**, and the reason is §10.6's audit
// rather than a style choice: that audit walks every focus stop in the **served**
// document, and a control rendered by this package rather than by the render
// pipeline gets no `.target` from `internal/content/target.go` — that pass adds
// the class to a *page body's* focusable elements and this is editor chrome, not
// a page body. A class applied from the script that arrives with the behaviour
// would be invisible to the walk.
//
// `type="button"` and no `formaction`, for the same reason the save control is
// one: the request is a `PUT` with an `If-Match`, and a form can express neither
// the method nor the header. The control therefore goes nowhere on its own, which
// is the correct server-rendered half of a control whose other half is phase 9's
// script.
templ calloutControl(callout CalloutView, view DisclosuresView) {
	<li
		class="secret-disclosure"
		data-secret={ callout.State.String() }
		data-secret-ordinal={ strconv.Itoa(callout.Ordinal) }
		data-testid={ CalloutControlTestID + "-" + strconv.Itoa(callout.Ordinal) }
	>
		<span class="secret-disclosure-name">{ callout.Name() }</span>
		if callout.State == StateRevealed {
			<span class="secret-disclosure-state" data-testid={ CalloutControlTestID + "-" + strconv.Itoa(callout.Ordinal) + "-state" }>
				{ markerWord }
			</span>
		}
		if callout.State == StateHidden {
			<button
				type="button"
				class={ ui.TargetClass + " button button--secret" }
				{ RevealHook }={ callout.Anchor }
				data-secret-href={ view.Endpoint }
				data-secret-if-match={ view.Validator }
				aria-label={ controlLabel(revealLabel, callout, view.Page) }
				data-testid={ CalloutControlTestID + "-" + strconv.Itoa(callout.Ordinal) + "-reveal" }
			>
				{ revealLabel }
			</button>
		} else {
			<button
				type="button"
				class={ ui.TargetClass + " button" }
				{ UnrevealHook }={ callout.Anchor }
				data-secret-href={ view.Endpoint }
				data-secret-if-match={ view.Validator }
				aria-label={ controlLabel(hideLabel, callout, view.Page) }
				data-testid={ CalloutControlTestID + "-" + strconv.Itoa(callout.Ordinal) + "-unreveal" }
			>
				{ hideLabel }
			</button>
		}
	</li>
}

// controlLabel is one control's accessible name: the action, the callout, and the
// page.
//
// All three, and the page is not decoration. A GM with three editor tabs open is
// looking at three "Reveal" buttons, and "Reveal this secret" three times over
// says nothing about which page any of them acts on.
func controlLabel(action string, callout CalloutView, page string) string {
	if page == "" {
		return action + ": " + callout.Name()
	}

	return action + ": " + callout.Name() + " on " + page
}

// revealAllControl is the page-level control, and the confirmation it needs.
//
// §4.10.3 gives the count as the requirement: "with a confirmation naming the
// count. One click rewrites N markers". The count is in the hook **and** in the
// accessible name, rather than in a `confirm()` call, because `confirm()` is a
// browser dialog no assistive technology can be told about in advance and because
// a synchronous modal from a `type="button"` is what §7.7 says to avoid in the
// editor. The confirmation is a real control the script shows, with the count
// available to both.
//
// **Its endpoint and its validator are its own**, on the same two attributes every
// per-callout control carries, so the page-level reveal is not a different kind of
// request from the single one — it is N of them, each with the same precondition.
templ revealAllControl(view DisclosuresView) {
	<p class="secret-disclosure-all">
		<button
			type="button"
			class={ ui.TargetClass + " button" }
			{ RevealAllHook }={ strconv.Itoa(len(view.Callouts)) }
			data-secret-href={ view.Endpoint }
			data-secret-if-match={ view.Validator }
			aria-label={ revealAllLabel + ": " + strconv.Itoa(len(view.Callouts)) + " on " + view.Page }
			data-testid={ RevealAllTestID }
		>
			{ revealAllLabel }
		</button>
	</p>
}

// RevealOutcomeView is what a reveal did.
//
// **A closed set rather than a caller-supplied sentence**, and the reason is the
// whole shape of this surface: the copy is the interface's, and the *state* is
// the server's. A caller that could pass its own sentence could pass one
// containing a secret, and this is the one component whose text reaches a
// document a player might later read.
type RevealOutcomeView struct {
	// Anchor is the callout the outcome is about, used in the heading so a GM
	// with four tabs knows which. It is a block id or twelve hex characters —
	// never the callout's body, and §5.6.3's `Resolve` is what makes a
	// derived anchor stable enough to speak aloud.
	Anchor string
	// Revealed is which way it went.
	Revealed bool
}

// RevealOutcome is the fragment a reveal's result is patched into.
//
// **`role="alert" aria-live="assertive"`**, and the reasoning is the package
// comment's: the GM pressed a button that made something public to every member
// and needs to know now whether it took effect. A silent failure here is a secret
// the GM believes is public and a party that was never told, which is the worst
// outcome in §5.6.
//
// It is a **fragment**, not the region. The region is `OutcomeRegion`, empty at
// load, and §7.5's mechanics are that a live region present on arrival announces
// nothing — so the alert is an *insertion*, which is what makes it speak.
//
// **`data-secret-outcome` carries which way it went**, and it is load-bearing in
// two directions: `secret.css` selects on it to frame a hide differently from a
// reveal, and a test asserts the attribute is present — so a stylesheet written
// against a value the markup does not carry is a test that fails rather than a
// product that renders unstyled.
templ RevealOutcome(view RevealOutcomeView) {
	if view.Revealed {
		<p
			class="secret-outcome"
			role="alert"
			aria-live="assertive"
			data-secret-outcome="revealed"
			data-testid={ OutcomeTestID }
		>
			{ revealedSentence }
		</p>
	} else {
		<p
			class="secret-outcome"
			role="alert"
			aria-live="assertive"
			data-secret-outcome="hidden"
			data-testid={ OutcomeTestID }
		>
			{ hideSentence }
		</p>
	}
}

// OutcomeRegion is the assertive region a reveal's outcome is patched into.
//
// **Empty in the served document, on purpose**, and the comment earns its length
// because "why is there an empty div" is the question a reader will have. §7.5:
// a live region announces on *transition*, and a GM who opens the editor has not
// transitioned — they have arrived. A region with content at load would either
// announce a stale outcome or need a `aria-live="off"` sibling, and the editor
// already has that pattern (`live.NoticePanel`) for the conditions it knows at
// load.
//
// The container is present either way because a region that is absent cannot be
// patched into, and the heading is a sibling rather than a child so that replacing
// the region's contents does not replace the region's name.
//
// **No `tabindex` and no focusable descendant**, for §7.5's rule that a patch
// must never touch the focused element: the way to guarantee that is for the
// target to be incapable of holding focus. `TestNoPatchTargetCanHoldFocus`
// asserts the property rather than the absence of an attribute.
templ OutcomeRegion() {
	<div class="secret-outcomes">
		<h3 id={ outcomeHeadingID() } class="visually-hidden">What a reveal did</h3>
		<div
			class="secret-outcome-region"
			role="alert"
			aria-live="assertive"
			aria-labelledby={ outcomeHeadingID() }
			{ ChromeAttribute }={ OutcomeHook }
			data-testid={ OutcomeRegionTestID }
		></div>
	</div>
}

// CappedAlertView is §4.10.3's reconcile-capped banner: the GM is told the system
// chose to hide something.
//
// `Detail` is a `string` rather than nothing, because the condition has a cause a
// GM can act on — a sync client fighting the reveal — and a banner that says only
// "a secret stayed hidden" leaves them with nothing to do about it. S-5.10 is
// the requirement: the cap leaves the secret hidden, and a GM who does not hear
// that will keep believing it is public.
type CappedAlertView struct {
	// Detail is the one line under the heading. Empty renders no paragraph,
	// which is the right outcome for a condition needing no elaboration.
	Detail string
}

// CappedAlert is the reconcile-capped fragment, patched into `live`'s assertive
// region.
//
// **`role="alert"` and `aria-live="assertive"`, written out.** §4.10.3 calls it an
// error-level event because the system *chose* to hide something; §7.5's
// announced-content table lists "Secret reveal, reconcile-capped" as
// `role="alert"`. The implicit politeness of the role is already assertive, so
// the attribute is redundant to a browser and load-bearing to a reader of this
// markup — the same reasoning `ui.RulesetDrift` gives.
//
// A `<p>` and no heading: it goes into `live`'s region, which already carries a
// `role="alert"`, so a heading here would be inside an announcement and would be
// read as part of it. `live.AnnouncementFragment` sets out the same rule.
templ CappedAlert(view CappedAlertView) {
	<p class="secret-capped" role="alert" aria-live="assertive" data-testid={ CappedTestID }>
		<strong>{ cappedHeading }</strong>
		if view.Detail != "" {
			{ " " + view.Detail }
		}
	</p>
}

// CalloutChrome is a callout's own chrome: its state, and the "Revealed" marker.
//
// **The marker is a `<span>` holding a word, not a class and not a colour**, and
// §4.10.4 gives the reason: "it is the one place the UI must be understood
// without sight *or* without colour perception". The class carries the styling;
// the text carries the meaning, and it survives `forced-colors` and a
// monochrome display because it is text.
//
// **No `role` anywhere on this element**, and there are two reasons it is worth
// stating rather than leaving. A marker is a label a GM reads while scanning a
// page, not an event, so a live region would be §7.5's replay failure — a
// re-render that speaks. And a `role="group"` on an unnamed container is a
// landmark-shaped region that names nothing, which §4.6's "absent rather than
// empty" is about. The callout's title carries the name; this element groups
// nothing a reader navigates by.
//
// The `<h3>` above it is the route's, not this component's: a callout's title is
// a `h3` inside a wiki page's `h2`-level outline, and a component that rendered
// its own heading would have to be told which level — a field a caller can be
// passed the wrong value for, which is the reason this package has no wrapper.
templ CalloutChrome(view CalloutChromeView) {
	<div class="secret-chrome" data-secret={ view.State.String() } data-testid={ CalloutChromeTestID + "-" + strconv.Itoa(view.Ordinal) }>
		<span class="secret-chrome-title">{ view.Title }</span>
		if view.Marker == MarkerShown && view.State == StateRevealed {
			<span class="secret-marker" data-testid={ MarkerTestID }>
				{ markerWord }
			</span>
		}
	</div>
}

// CalloutChromeView is one callout's chrome.
type CalloutChromeView struct {
	// Title is the callout's header line. Rendered as text and escaped by
	// templ; it is author-supplied and reachable through Obsidian Sync
	// (AGENTS.md), so it is never interpolated unescaped.
	Title string
	// Ordinal is the callout's position, used in the test hook. Never rendered
	// visibly: a GM scanning a page does not want a number, and a screen
	// reader gets the title from the row that names the control.
	Ordinal int
	// State is which way the marker goes.
	State State
	// Marker decides whether the "Revealed" label is rendered. `MarkerOff` is
	// the published page's default and the zero value.
	Marker Marker
}

// StubView is the §4.10.2 opt-in stub: an empty blockquote carrying one
// non-secret label.
//
// **Per-campaign and off by default**, and the default is the whole of §5.6.1's
// argument: a stub discloses the *existence and position* of a secret, which in
// a game about who is hiding what is itself information. So `Stub` exists, is
// rendered only when a caller supplies a non-empty `StubView`, and a caller that
// forgot to is in the safe state.
//
// It is a `<blockquote>`, not a `<div class="secret">`, because the rendered
// callout's class allowlist is `internal/content/policy.go`'s and a `div` carrying
// it is a class the sanitiser would strip; and because a stub is not a callout —
// it has no marker byte, no state, and nothing to reveal. A `blockquote` reads as
// a quotation, which is what a *note to the GM* is.
//
// **`data-stub`, and deliberately not `data-secret-stub`.** The `data-secret*` family
// is this package's convention for "this names a callout or a control for one", and
// the audit over a player's document refuses the whole family. A stub names
// nothing — no anchor, no ordinal, no state — so putting it in that family would
// make the audit's family assertion untrue for the one element that legitimately
// carries no identity at all.
templ Stub(view StubView) {
	if view.Enabled {
		<blockquote class="secret-stub" data-stub data-testid={ StubTestID }>
			{ stubSentence }
		</blockquote>
	}
}

// StubView decides whether a redacted callout leaves a trace.
type StubView struct {
	// Enabled is the campaign's opt-in. **False is the zero value and the
	// default**, and §5.6.1's reason is the reason: the stub discloses that a
	// secret was here.
	Enabled bool
}